// A real, generic two-value bundle -- see `IDEAS.md`'s own note (buried inside `COMPREHENSIONS_
// IDEAS.md`'s "Explicitly deferred" list: "this language has no tuple type at all today"). Opt-in
// via `use tuple;`. Needed ZERO new compiler machinery: `Tuple2<A, B>` is an ordinary two-type-
// param generic struct, exactly the same shape `stdlib/collections.hotc`'s own `Entry2<K, V>`
// already proved out this session -- no new parser/checker/codegen work at all.
//
// ```
// use tuple;
//
// fn main() {
//     let p = tuple2(3, "three");
//     print(p.item0);   // 3
//     print(p.item1);   // "three"
//
//     // Directly unblocks a real comprehension result this language previously couldn't
//     // express at all (`COMPREHENSIONS_IDEAS.md`'s own deferred "tuple results" item) --
//     // `result_expr` was always allowed to be ANY expression, including an ordinary call, so
//     // this needed nothing new on the comprehension side either:
//     let pairs: Vec<Tuple2<Int, Int>> = [tuple2(x, x * x) for x in [1, 2, 3]];
// }
// ```
//
// Real, disclosed scope cut: no `(a, b)` LITERAL syntax this pass (a real parser/AST addition,
// not attempted here) -- `tuple2(a, b)` is the real, available constructor. Scoped to TWO values
// only: a real third type parameter on `StructDecl` (`Tuple3<A, B, C>`) isn't supported by this
// compiler's own generics machinery today (`type_param`/`type_param2` cap out at two) -- worth
// its own separate extension if a real caller needs three, not attempted speculatively here.
struct Tuple2<A, B> {
    item0: A,
    item1: B,
}

fn tuple2<A, B>(a: A, b: B) -> Tuple2<A, B> {
    return Tuple2<A, B> { item0: a, item1: b };
}
